6.) DATABASES AND SQL BASICS


DATABASES 

-> Database is a container filled with data and information whic is stored in a computer system.
Data in any form can be stored in the database.
The main purpose to store is to have easy accessibility, to keep it protected , analyse it etc.

SQL 

Structured Query Language

-> It is used for communincating between databases allowing us to
insert, delete , modify data

SQL Queries -> Commands or instructions given to databse to modify, delete etc etc data.

In SQL, There is something called CRUD Operations
C -> used to insert new records in table.
R -> used to retrieve data from table.
U -> used to update data in a table.
D -> used to delete data froma  table.

SQL Queries Commands
A.)  SELECT STATEMENT

-> SELECT is used to retrieve data from a database.
-> It allows us to specify the columns we want to retrieve.
-> The WHERE clause can be used to filter the data.

Syntax:

SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name
WHERE condition;

-> The condition in the WHERE clause acts as a filter.
-> It specifies which rows should be included in the result.

Example:

SELECT name, age
FROM students
WHERE age > 18;

-> This retrieves the name and age of students whose age is greater than 18.

IMPORTANT:

-> In most SQL database management systems, an SQL statement ends with a semicolon ;.

B.) INSERT STATEMENT

-> INSERT is used to add new records to a table.
-> We specify the values for each column in the new row.

Syntax:

INSERT INTO table_name (column1, column2)
VALUES (value1, value2);

Example:

INSERT INTO students (name, age)
VALUES ('Arnav', 18);

C.) UPDATE STATEMENT

-> UPDATE is used to modify existing records in a table.
-> It allows us to change the values of specific columns based on certain conditions.

Syntax:

UPDATE table_name
SET column1 = value1
WHERE condition;

Example:

UPDATE students
SET age = 19
WHERE name = 'Arnav';

D.) DELETE STATEMENT

-> DELETE is used to remove records from a table based on specified conditions.

Syntax:

DELETE FROM table_name
WHERE condition;

Example:

DELETE FROM students
WHERE name = 'Arnav';

E.) JOIN

-> JOIN is used to combine rows from two or more tables.
-> The tables are combined based on a related column.

Types of JOINs:

-> INNER JOIN
-> LEFT JOIN
-> RIGHT JOIN
-> FULL JOIN

Basic syntax:

SELECT *
FROM table1
JOIN table2
ON table1.column_name = table2.column_name;

-> table1 and table2 are the tables being joined.
-> column_name is the common/related column used for the join.

-> JOINs allow us to combine data from different tables and extract meaningful information from a relational database.

    DATA TYPES

-> Each column in a SQL table has a data type.
-> The data type defines the type of data that the column can store.

Common data types:

INTEGER

-> Used for whole numbers.

Example:

age INTEGER

VARCHAR

-> Used for variable-length text.

Example:

name VARCHAR

DATE

