11. CONSTRAINTS

-> Constraints are rules defined on tables.
-> They help maintain data integrity.

Common constraints:

PRIMARY KEY

-> Ensures uniqueness of a column's values.
-> Used to uniquely identify records.

FOREIGN KEY

-> Maintains referential integrity between two tables.
-> Used to create relationships between tables.

NOT NULL

-> Ensures that a column must have a value.

UNIQUE

-> Ensures that values in a column are unique.

12. INDEXES

-> Indexes are database objects used to speed up data retrieval.
-> They allow the database system to find and access data more quickly.
-> They are especially useful for large tables.

13. AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS

-> Aggregate functions perform calculations on a set of data.
-> They return a single result.

Common aggregate functions:

SUM()

-> Calculates the total.

SELECT SUM(marks)
FROM students;
AVG()

-> Calculates the average.

SELECT AVG(marks)
FROM students;
COUNT()

-> Counts records.

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM students;
MIN()

-> Finds the minimum value.

SELECT MIN(marks)
FROM students;
MAX()

-> Finds the maximum value.

SELECT MAX(marks)
FROM students;
14. GROUP BY

-> GROUP BY is used to group data based on specific columns.
-> It is commonly used with aggregate functions such as SUM() and COUNT().
-> It is useful for generating summary results.

Example:

SELECT city, COUNT(*)
FROM students
GROUP BY city;

-> This counts the number of students in each city.

IMPORTANT:

-> When using GROUP BY, the SELECT statement should include either:

Columns used for grouping, or
Aggregate functions used to calculate values within each group.
15. ORDER BY

-> ORDER BY is used to sort the result set.
-> It can sort data in ascending or descending order.

Syntax:

SELECT *
FROM table_name
ORDER BY column_name ASC;

Ascending:

ASC

Descending:

DESC

Example:

SELECT *
FROM students
ORDER BY age DESC;

-> This sorts students by age from highest to lowest.

16. GROUP BY + COUNT() + ORDER BY

These can be combined to create useful summary results.

Example:

SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS count_alias
FROM students
GROUP BY city
ORDER BY count_alias DESC;

-> COUNT(*) counts the number of rows in each group.
-> AS count_alias gives a custom name to the result.
-> GROUP BY city creates groups based on city.
-> ORDER BY count_alias DESC sorts the groups from highest count to lowest count.

17. SUBQUERY

-> A subquery is also called an inner query or nested query.
-> It is a query embedded inside another query.
-> It can be used to retrieve data that will be used as a filter or condition in the main query.

Basic idea:

Main Query
    │
    └── Subquery
          │
          └── Provides data/condition

-> The main advantage of a subquery is that it allows complex filtering and data retrieval in a more organized manner.

18. VIEWS

-> A View is a virtual table created using a query.
-> It can be used like a regular table.
-> Views can simplify complex queries.
-> They can also help restrict access to sensitive data.

Basic syntax:

CREATE VIEW view_name AS
SELECT column1, column2
FROM table_name
WHERE condition;
19. TRANSACTIONS

-> A transaction is a sequence of one or more SQL operations treated as a single unit.

-> The purpose of a transaction is to ensure that a series of related database operations either:

All succeed, or
All fail.

-> Transactions help maintain data integrity and consistency.

Example idea:

Transaction
    │
    ├── Operation 1
    ├── Operation 2
    └── Operation 3
          │
          ▼
    All succeed
          OR
    All fail
20. ACID PROPERTIES

The reliability of transactions is commonly described using ACID properties.

A → Atomicity
C → Consistency
I → Isolation
D → Durability

Atomicity

-> A transaction is treated as a single unit.
-> Either all operations happen or none happen.

Consistency

-> The database remains in a valid and consistent state.

Isolation

-> Transactions are isolated from each other while they are being executed.

Durability

-> Once a transaction is successfully completed, its changes are preserved.

21. NORMALIZATION

-> Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database.
-> Its purpose is to:

Eliminate redundancy
Improve data integrity

-> It involves dividing a database into tables and defining relationships between them.

Example idea:

Instead of unnecessarily repeating the same student information in multiple places, related information can be separated into appropriate tables and connected using keys.

22. STORED PROCEDURES

-> Stored Procedures are precompiled SQL code that can be stored and executed on the database server.
-> They can help with performance and security.

Basic idea:

Application
     │
     ▼
Stored Procedure
     │
     ▼
Database Operations
23. TRIGGERS

-> A Trigger is a database object that automatically executes in response to a specific event.
-> It can be used when data is modified.
-> Triggers can help maintain data consistency and integrity.

Common triggering events:

-> INSERT
-> UPDATE
-> DELETE

Triggers can execute:

BEFORE an event
AFTER an event
INSTEAD OF an event (in certain cases such as views)

Basic structure:

CREATE [OR REPLACE] TRIGGER trigger_name
{BEFORE | AFTER | INSTEAD OF}
{INSERT | UPDATE | DELETE}
ON table_name
[FOR EACH ROW]

BEGIN
    -- SQL statements
END;
Important parts:

trigger_name
-> Name given to the trigger.

BEFORE
-> Trigger executes before the event.

AFTER
-> Trigger executes after the event.

INSTEAD OF
-> Trigger executes instead of the triggering event, used in certain cases such as views.

INSERT | UPDATE | DELETE
-> Specifies which action activates the trigger.

FOR EACH ROW
-> Trigger fires for each affected row.

BEGIN ... END
-> Contains the SQL statements that execute when the trigger is activated.
